  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">The handoff, step by step</h2>
      <p class="reveal">The introduction is what we do. We do not buy your records, hold a copy of them, or resell them later: the licence is signed with a data buyer, and the company is paid by that buyer.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>We make the introduction</strong>Once a company clears the published floors, we put it in front of the buyers whose criteria it meets and stay in the conversation while diligence runs.</li>
        <li><strong>The buyer runs diligence</strong>Rights, personal data, scope and term are reviewed by the buyer, in its own process, before any number is put in writing.</li>
        <li><strong>You approve, then sign</strong>The scope, the deletion date, the audit right and the payment schedule sit in the agreement with the buyer. Your company signs it, not us.</li>
        <li><strong>The buyer pays you</strong>Payment goes to the company directly, on the schedule in that agreement. We are paid by the buyer for the introduction, so nothing is deducted from what you receive.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What slows a deal down</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Most of the delay sits in the review stages, and it is usually paperwork rather than interest. These are the ones that come up most.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Contracts that say nothing about third-party use</strong>If the customer agreement is silent on whether records can be licensed, the buyer has to resolve that before it can proceed.</li>
        <li><strong>Data that cannot be exported cleanly</strong>Records trapped in a tool with no usable export, or spread across systems with no shared identifier, take longer to scope.</li>
        <li><strong>Personal information mixed through the records</strong>Names, contact details and free-text notes need review and transformation, which is separate work from the rights review.</li>
        <li><strong>A scope that keeps moving</strong>Adding categories after diligence has started restarts part of it, which is why the scope is agreed before delivery rather than during it.</li>
        <li><strong>The buyer&rsquo;s own priorities</strong>The buyer takes the agreed scope to the AI lab that would use it, and a lab can change what it is buying.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="reveal">A no is a normal outcome, and it arrives on the first call rather than after months of review. The <a href="what-affects-a-deal.html">terms that move a number</a> are worth reading before that call, because they also decide how quickly it can close.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

